WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.401
I'm Mike Massimino, a
former NASA astronaut.

00:00:02.401 --> 00:00:04.774
I was with NASA for 18 years,

00:00:04.774 --> 00:00:07.330
and my primary job was to be a spacewalker

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:08.540
on the Hubble Space Telescope.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:12.460
And this is everything I'm
authorized to tell you.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:13.885
Everyone says, "Adios,"

00:00:13.885 --> 00:00:16.250
and then they close the
inner hatch and lock it.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.958
So now they're getting rid
of all the air in the airlock

00:00:18.958 --> 00:00:20.988
until it goes down to vacuum.

00:00:20.988 --> 00:00:23.466
Even though I'd been in
space for a couple days,

00:00:23.466 --> 00:00:24.901
I felt there was a really big difference

00:00:24.901 --> 00:00:26.877
between being inside of a spaceship

00:00:26.877 --> 00:00:28.923
and going outside of it.

00:00:28.923 --> 00:00:31.273
You know, like, that's
space out there, man.

00:00:31.273 --> 00:00:33.342
The only thing that's
protecting me is my spacesuit.

00:00:33.342 --> 00:00:34.896
And it was like this door, you know?

00:00:34.896 --> 00:00:36.715
It's the door to space.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:44.264
In the shuttle days, we had
two categories of astronauts.

00:00:44.264 --> 00:00:47.958
We had astronaut pilots who
were military test pilots

00:00:47.958 --> 00:00:50.087
or former military test pilots.

00:00:50.087 --> 00:00:51.124
And their primary job

00:00:51.124 --> 00:00:53.528
was to operate and fly
the vehicle, land it,

00:00:53.528 --> 00:00:55.238
because it landed like a glider

00:00:55.238 --> 00:00:57.140
or like an aircraft on a runway.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:58.487
I was a mission specialist.

00:00:58.487 --> 00:01:01.278
My job was to do all the other stuff.

00:01:01.278 --> 00:01:03.251
Do the space walks, work the robot arm,

00:01:03.251 --> 00:01:04.581
do the experiments,

00:01:04.581 --> 00:01:06.519
help with the systems
of the space shuttle.

00:01:06.519 --> 00:01:09.648
My primary responsibility on
both of my flights, though,

00:01:09.648 --> 00:01:11.194
was to be a spacewalker.

00:01:11.194 --> 00:01:12.958
My first mission, we trained
for a year and a half.

00:01:12.958 --> 00:01:13.791
My second year,

00:01:13.791 --> 00:01:15.040
we ended up training for
two and a half years.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:16.407
It was only supposed to
be a year and a half.

00:01:16.407 --> 00:01:19.458
You're training the whole time,
as far as NASA is concerned,

00:01:19.458 --> 00:01:21.698
you're hired as an astronaut candidate

00:01:21.698 --> 00:01:24.018
or as the experienced
people like to call you,

00:01:24.018 --> 00:01:26.326
you're an ASCAN, or we are the ASCANs.

00:01:26.326 --> 00:01:27.834
We need something done around here.

00:01:27.834 --> 00:01:29.966
And you don't get that title astronaut

00:01:29.966 --> 00:01:33.098
until you complete the training program.

00:01:33.098 --> 00:01:35.061
And then you successfully
graduate from that.

00:01:35.061 --> 00:01:36.508
It usually takes about two years,

00:01:36.508 --> 00:01:38.248
and you get a silver astronaut pin.

00:01:38.248 --> 00:01:40.157
And then when you fly in space,

00:01:40.157 --> 00:01:41.946
you get a gold astronaut pin.

00:01:41.946 --> 00:01:45.411
So after being at NASA for four years,

00:01:45.411 --> 00:01:46.978
I was assigned to my first flight.

00:01:46.978 --> 00:01:48.955
And then it was about another
year and a half after that

00:01:48.955 --> 00:01:50.622
until I flew that flight.

00:01:50.622 --> 00:01:52.899
For Hubble, it was learning about systems

00:01:52.899 --> 00:01:54.829
and also learning more about

00:01:54.829 --> 00:01:57.060
how the shuttle is going
to be used for the flight.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:58.285
And then the space walk.

00:01:58.285 --> 00:01:59.923
You train specific space walks

00:01:59.923 --> 00:02:04.030
and specific robotic
operations for your flight.

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:05.588
There's different facilities to do that,

00:02:05.588 --> 00:02:07.375
where, like, there's an air-bearing floor

00:02:07.375 --> 00:02:09.254
where you're moving things around.

00:02:09.254 --> 00:02:10.637
It's kind like an air hockey table,

00:02:10.637 --> 00:02:12.629
so the thing kind of floats,
and you're moving around.

00:02:12.629 --> 00:02:16.121
But it all kind of comes
together when you go to the pool.

00:02:16.121 --> 00:02:17.813
You'd get into your space suit

00:02:17.813 --> 00:02:19.365
and be put in the pool,

00:02:19.365 --> 00:02:21.001
and they would weigh you out.

00:02:21.001 --> 00:02:23.437
Meaning that they would get
the right amount of flotation

00:02:23.437 --> 00:02:26.299
and weights for you to
be a neutrally buoyant.

00:02:26.299 --> 00:02:28.230
Meaning the force of gravity

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:30.189
and the buoyancy force of
the water kind of evens out,

00:02:30.189 --> 00:02:32.384
and you're floating in the water column,

00:02:32.384 --> 00:02:34.400
similar to the way you'll
be floating in space.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.221
And then you run the space
walk from start to finish.

00:02:37.221 --> 00:02:40.591
100 feet wide, it's 200 feet
long, and it's 40 feet deep.

00:02:40.591 --> 00:02:41.756
It is a really big pool.

00:02:41.756 --> 00:02:43.849
So the Hubble missions,

00:02:43.849 --> 00:02:46.860
they used to call us the prima donna crew,

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.387
and everyone in the
office would would call,

00:02:49.387 --> 00:02:51.252
"Oh, you're prima donnas."

00:02:51.252 --> 00:02:53.243
And I liked that.

00:02:53.243 --> 00:02:56.730
That was cool, because
yes, we are special.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:58.029
And the reason they called us that

00:02:58.029 --> 00:03:00.135
is because of the support we had.

00:03:00.135 --> 00:03:02.308
It was a huge team of people,

00:03:02.308 --> 00:03:03.937
not only at the Johnson Space Center,

00:03:03.937 --> 00:03:06.426
but also from the Goddard
Space Flight Center.

00:03:06.426 --> 00:03:08.166
So we had a lot of support.

00:03:08.166 --> 00:03:10.706
So back in the day when we
were doing lots of space walks,

00:03:10.706 --> 00:03:13.161
building a space station,
and going to Hubble,

00:03:13.161 --> 00:03:15.057
they would have what we called dual ops.

00:03:15.057 --> 00:03:16.987
So you would have two crews in there.

00:03:16.987 --> 00:03:20.665
The training in the pool was really good

00:03:20.665 --> 00:03:23.082
to get you ready to do your job.

00:03:23.082 --> 00:03:25.804
I felt very prepared when I got to space.

00:03:25.804 --> 00:03:27.173
At the end of my first space walk,

00:03:27.173 --> 00:03:29.608
I remember closing an equipment bay door

00:03:29.608 --> 00:03:31.349
and realizing that I felt like

00:03:31.349 --> 00:03:33.296
I had done that task 100 times,

00:03:33.296 --> 00:03:35.963
even though I had never been in space.

00:03:35.963 --> 00:03:39.329
Toilet on the shuttle
required some training

00:03:39.329 --> 00:03:41.158
to just get your aim correctly

00:03:41.158 --> 00:03:43.287
because it was a small
hole on the commode.

00:03:43.287 --> 00:03:44.650
So you would pee into a hose

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:46.480
and that was pretty easy to do that.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.676
But the commode on the shuttle

00:03:49.676 --> 00:03:52.910
was a bit complicated
in a very small opening.

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:56.227
So the deal was you had to
practice your alignment.

00:03:56.227 --> 00:03:59.712
And the way that worked is we
had, in our simulator area,

00:03:59.712 --> 00:04:02.263
we had a toilet that worked
just like the shuttle toilet.

00:04:02.263 --> 00:04:04.181
And so you could practice on that one.

00:04:04.181 --> 00:04:05.584
You could poop into that thing.

00:04:05.584 --> 00:04:08.000
You made sure you call them to tell them,

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.653
"Hey, it's time to come service that."

00:04:09.653 --> 00:04:11.277
'Because it wasn't a flush toilet.

00:04:11.277 --> 00:04:13.061
It would contain the material.

00:04:13.061 --> 00:04:13.894
So to practice for that,

00:04:13.894 --> 00:04:15.258
there was another toilet right next to it

00:04:15.258 --> 00:04:16.821
that you did not poop in.

00:04:16.821 --> 00:04:18.402
But that was like an alignment trainer

00:04:18.402 --> 00:04:22.681
and that had a camera
looking up through the hole.

00:04:22.681 --> 00:04:25.724
And it had a close
circuit TV right over here

00:04:25.724 --> 00:04:28.610
that they promised that that
was not going anywhere else.

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:32.221
And then memorize that
position on the toilet,

00:04:32.221 --> 00:04:33.853
like, you know, I felt,
oh, I'm riding a chop,

00:04:33.853 --> 00:04:35.003
you know, and a motorcycle.

00:04:35.003 --> 00:04:37.039
I felt like, all right, that was a feeling

00:04:37.039 --> 00:04:38.139
of kind of leaning back.

00:04:38.139 --> 00:04:39.476
And then you'd kind of memorize that

00:04:39.476 --> 00:04:41.422
and then you could actually try it out

00:04:41.422 --> 00:04:43.085
and see how well you did on the real one

00:04:43.085 --> 00:04:44.120
that was next to you.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:45.953
They don't usually tell
anybody about that.

00:04:45.953 --> 00:04:48.972
You know, it's like a deep
dark secret that you have:

00:04:48.972 --> 00:04:52.659
to align your butt
correctly over the commode.

00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:01.550
The night before my first
launch, I was pretty excited,

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:02.780
anticipating everything.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:04.351
You're in crew quarters with your crew.

00:05:04.351 --> 00:05:05.296
You're in quarantine.

00:05:05.296 --> 00:05:07.235
You do get able to see your family,

00:05:07.235 --> 00:05:08.607
whoever's cleared by the doctor.

00:05:08.607 --> 00:05:11.237
The night before, you get
to eat whatever you want.

00:05:11.237 --> 00:05:13.003
I remember, Lady Dot was her name,

00:05:13.003 --> 00:05:15.556
who was head of kind of
running crew quarters

00:05:15.556 --> 00:05:17.425
down there at the Kennedy
Space Center said to me

00:05:17.425 --> 00:05:18.648
they were going to go shopping

00:05:18.648 --> 00:05:20.406
and asked me what I wanted for dinner.

00:05:20.406 --> 00:05:21.640
I was like, "Whatever you have, Dot.

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:22.787
It's all good here. I don't care.

00:05:22.787 --> 00:05:24.263
She goes, "I think you can have
anything you want tonight."

00:05:24.263 --> 00:05:26.547
And I go, "Well, whatever
you have was fine with me."

00:05:26.547 --> 00:05:27.998
And she looks at me and grabs my hand.

00:05:27.998 --> 00:05:30.649
She goes, "Honey, you're going
on a rocket ship tomorrow.

00:05:30.649 --> 00:05:32.585
You can have anything
you want to eat tonight."

00:05:32.585 --> 00:05:34.542
And I was like, "OK,
Dot, how about a steak?"

00:05:34.542 --> 00:05:37.558
So you wake up and you get dressed.

00:05:37.558 --> 00:05:39.682
Usually you have a crew shirt picked out

00:05:39.682 --> 00:05:40.927
because you're going to
get your picture taken

00:05:40.927 --> 00:05:42.157
as a crew at breakfast.

00:05:42.157 --> 00:05:43.849
There's a cake there.

00:05:43.849 --> 00:05:45.191
That's a kind of a
tradition to have a cake

00:05:45.191 --> 00:05:46.702
whether or not you want
a piece of cake or not,

00:05:46.702 --> 00:05:48.562
but they'll have a cake there for you.

00:05:48.562 --> 00:05:50.708
Every crew member has a
technician helping them

00:05:50.708 --> 00:05:53.831
that you've trained with
throughout your training flow

00:05:53.831 --> 00:05:56.957
whenever you have the suit
on for your training events.

00:05:56.957 --> 00:05:58.088
And so they're there

00:05:58.088 --> 00:05:59.640
and you get the suit on,

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:01.223
get your pocket stuffed with whatever.

00:06:01.223 --> 00:06:03.612
The commander plays a hand of poker

00:06:03.612 --> 00:06:05.352
with the head of the office,

00:06:05.352 --> 00:06:06.907
the head of the astronaut office,

00:06:06.907 --> 00:06:08.763
until the commander loses

00:06:08.763 --> 00:06:10.494
to leave all your bad luck on the planet.

00:06:10.494 --> 00:06:11.872
Charlie said, "Mike, come
over here and join us."

00:06:11.872 --> 00:06:16.872
And I played poker with Charlie
and Scooter, our commander.

00:06:16.889 --> 00:06:19.739
And one of our crew mates was
like, "Whoa, what's going on?

00:06:19.739 --> 00:06:20.572
This is bad luck.

00:06:20.572 --> 00:06:22.002
You can't have an extra guy in there."

00:06:22.002 --> 00:06:25.110
Scooter eventually lost and
we headed to the launchpad.

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:26.667
For me, the biggest moment of fear

00:06:26.667 --> 00:06:28.084
was before my first launch,

00:06:28.084 --> 00:06:31.255
looking at the spaceship in the night sky.

00:06:31.255 --> 00:06:33.323
It's fully fueled and making noises

00:06:33.323 --> 00:06:35.660
and water vapors coming off of it,

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:37.890
but you know, it looks like
there's smoke coming off of it

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:40.264
and it looked a little intimidating.

00:06:40.264 --> 00:06:41.989
So I thought maybe this
wasn't such a good idea,

00:06:41.989 --> 00:06:43.750
but by then it's too
late and you have to go.

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:46.512
So the shuttle had a
flight deck with windows

00:06:46.512 --> 00:06:48.229
and instruments and stuff to look at

00:06:48.229 --> 00:06:50.065
and there was four people up there.

00:06:50.065 --> 00:06:51.433
And then there were three
of us on the mid deck,

00:06:51.433 --> 00:06:53.228
which really not much to look at,

00:06:53.228 --> 00:06:55.125
except these lockers in front of us.

00:06:55.125 --> 00:06:57.445
And we did have an altimeter
to tell us, you know,

00:06:57.445 --> 00:07:00.545
what the pressure was
inside of our spaceship.

00:07:00.545 --> 00:07:02.403
So all the action was on the flight deck.

00:07:02.403 --> 00:07:04.241
So we played like tic-tac-toe

00:07:04.241 --> 00:07:06.693
and rock, paper, scissors
sitting next to each other,

00:07:06.693 --> 00:07:07.526
that kind of thing.

00:07:07.526 --> 00:07:08.368
Then it's a couple minutes,

00:07:08.368 --> 00:07:10.685
and then it's one minute and 30 seconds.

00:07:10.685 --> 00:07:12.925
And then the spaceship
is starting to come alive

00:07:12.925 --> 00:07:13.758
at that point.

00:07:13.758 --> 00:07:14.673
With a couple minutes to go,

00:07:14.673 --> 00:07:16.832
they start up the auxiliary power units.

00:07:16.832 --> 00:07:19.543
And then six seconds, the engine start,

00:07:19.543 --> 00:07:22.367
and the whole stack leans
forward and then comes back.

00:07:22.367 --> 00:07:23.827
You still haven't gone anywhere yet

00:07:23.827 --> 00:07:26.167
until the solid rocket boosts
the two big white rockets

00:07:26.167 --> 00:07:27.360
on either side.

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:28.669
And once they light, you're moving.

00:07:28.669 --> 00:07:29.882
You haven't yet cleared the tower.

00:07:29.882 --> 00:07:30.924
You're still going a hundred miles an hour

00:07:30.924 --> 00:07:33.244
as soon as you take off and
you accelerate from zero

00:07:33.244 --> 00:07:36.639
to 17,500 miles an hour about
eight and a half minutes.

00:07:36.639 --> 00:07:38.183
You feel the acceleration.

00:07:38.183 --> 00:07:40.144
The g-force start to build up.

00:07:40.144 --> 00:07:42.113
There's a lot of shaking going on.

00:07:42.113 --> 00:07:43.548
With the solid rockets still attached,

00:07:43.548 --> 00:07:44.948
they run for two and a half minutes

00:07:44.948 --> 00:07:46.703
and they run rough because
there's solid material.

00:07:46.703 --> 00:07:50.009
The g-force build up to about 3G.

00:07:50.009 --> 00:07:53.300
So three times your body
weight is what you're feeling.

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:54.892
So I felt like there
were like three big dudes

00:07:54.892 --> 00:07:56.009
sitting on me.

00:07:56.009 --> 00:07:58.264
And then at the end of
eight and a half minutes,

00:07:58.264 --> 00:08:02.131
the main engine's cut and your weightless

00:08:02.131 --> 00:08:05.376
and then the feeling
is just weightlessness

00:08:05.376 --> 00:08:07.564
where there's no more forces on you.

00:08:07.564 --> 00:08:09.047
My arms just floated up like this

00:08:09.047 --> 00:08:10.686
because that happens naturally.

00:08:10.686 --> 00:08:12.582
You're kind of glad that you made it.

00:08:12.582 --> 00:08:13.664
Your first day, I didn't feel good.

00:08:13.664 --> 00:08:16.224
I threw up my first day in space.

00:08:16.224 --> 00:08:17.057
You get stomach awareness

00:08:17.057 --> 00:08:20.701
because your inner ear is trying to...

00:08:20.701 --> 00:08:21.896
Your brain's trying to
figure out what's going on

00:08:21.896 --> 00:08:23.261
because your inner ear works on gravity

00:08:23.261 --> 00:08:27.787
and there's no gravity when
you're in orbit affecting you.

00:08:27.787 --> 00:08:29.966
So your brain doesn't
know what's going on.

00:08:29.966 --> 00:08:32.161
And so it's a conflict between
the inner ear and the eyes,

00:08:32.161 --> 00:08:33.191
which leads to nausea.

00:08:33.191 --> 00:08:35.472
Sometimes sickness or air sickness.

00:08:35.472 --> 00:08:37.722
That kind of thing you might get in a car

00:08:37.722 --> 00:08:39.403
as, you know, car sickness.

00:08:39.403 --> 00:08:40.814
I was fine by the second day.

00:08:40.814 --> 00:08:41.647
My second flight,

00:08:41.647 --> 00:08:43.175
I think my brain remembered
to kicked right in

00:08:43.175 --> 00:08:44.797
and I was OK from the get go.

00:08:44.797 --> 00:08:47.095
But on day three is when we rendezvoused.

00:08:47.095 --> 00:08:50.053
You had a laser which
shoot off for distance

00:08:50.053 --> 00:08:51.595
and speed measurements

00:08:51.595 --> 00:08:53.630
and make sure all the
computers were working

00:08:53.630 --> 00:08:55.606
and we were getting closer.

00:08:55.606 --> 00:08:57.458
So you get it really
close to the telescope.

00:08:57.458 --> 00:09:00.156
You see it like a star in
the distance, you know,

00:09:00.156 --> 00:09:00.991
the beginning of the day

00:09:00.991 --> 00:09:02.475
and you're getting closer and closer.

00:09:02.475 --> 00:09:03.937
The way we grabbed the telescope

00:09:03.937 --> 00:09:06.680
is we would get it with the robot arm.

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:09.173
When you're docking with the
International Space Station,

00:09:09.173 --> 00:09:12.379
it's like a docking port
that docks with a...

00:09:12.379 --> 00:09:13.632
On, you know, one side of it

00:09:13.632 --> 00:09:16.151
on the spacecraft that's coming.

00:09:16.151 --> 00:09:17.988
And then the other side
on the space station.

00:09:17.988 --> 00:09:19.599
On Hubble, you had to grab it,

00:09:19.599 --> 00:09:21.246
and then we would go to a free drift,

00:09:21.246 --> 00:09:23.363
which means that no more
flying the spaceship.

00:09:23.363 --> 00:09:25.252
And that also means that you
don't have that much time.

00:09:25.252 --> 00:09:26.085
Because if you wait too long,

00:09:26.085 --> 00:09:27.237
the arm mechanics of the two vehicles

00:09:27.237 --> 00:09:28.513
will start to separate.

00:09:28.513 --> 00:09:31.822
So you know, you have a couple
minutes to go and get it.

00:09:31.822 --> 00:09:33.704
And then we'd place it
on a big turntable we had

00:09:33.704 --> 00:09:35.867
at the back of the space shuttle.

00:09:35.867 --> 00:09:39.577
So it would then be brought
down into those latches

00:09:39.577 --> 00:09:41.460
and we would latch it to the shuttle

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:43.262
and then we could remove the arm

00:09:43.262 --> 00:09:44.631
and then we would apply power to it.

00:09:44.631 --> 00:09:46.563
We had an umbilical
that would give it power

00:09:46.563 --> 00:09:48.579
and we could get data from it and so on.

00:09:48.579 --> 00:09:50.551
So that's what we would do.

00:09:50.551 --> 00:09:52.012
Then once it's attached,

00:09:52.012 --> 00:09:53.205
it's not going anywhere

00:09:53.205 --> 00:09:55.854
and it's powered so it's
going to stay alive.

00:09:55.854 --> 00:09:58.645
And then we're going to go start
our space walks on day four.

00:10:03.430 --> 00:10:05.769
Columbia was my first mission.

00:10:05.769 --> 00:10:08.205
It was in March of '02.

00:10:08.205 --> 00:10:11.453
The next launch for
Columbia was STS-107 flight

00:10:11.453 --> 00:10:14.120
and that was in January of 2003.

00:10:15.776 --> 00:10:17.385
On launch, they took some damage

00:10:17.385 --> 00:10:18.781
that they didn't know about.

00:10:18.781 --> 00:10:20.169
We didn't have a way to detect it,

00:10:20.169 --> 00:10:22.345
but they took a piece of
debris off the external tank,

00:10:22.345 --> 00:10:23.835
the big fuel tank.

00:10:23.835 --> 00:10:25.352
Put a hole in the wing,
which they didn't know about.

00:10:25.352 --> 00:10:27.187
We didn't find this out until after

00:10:27.187 --> 00:10:30.424
with the post-flight analysis.

00:10:30.424 --> 00:10:32.469
And when they tried to
reenter the atmosphere,

00:10:32.469 --> 00:10:33.518
it was a hole in the wing.

00:10:33.518 --> 00:10:36.480
He came in the wing, we
lost a vehicle in the crew.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:38.452
So that was a bad day.

00:10:38.452 --> 00:10:40.251
I knew them really well.

00:10:40.251 --> 00:10:42.601
Three of them were classmates of mine.

00:10:42.601 --> 00:10:44.110
The others, I knew really well.

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:46.396
Ilan Ramon was a Israeli astronaut

00:10:46.396 --> 00:10:49.667
who my family and I
became very close with.

00:10:49.667 --> 00:10:52.570
Still close with his kids.

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:53.891
And then the others, the other folks,

00:10:53.891 --> 00:10:55.487
Rick Husband was a very
good friend of mine,

00:10:55.487 --> 00:10:56.320
one of my better friends,

00:10:56.320 --> 00:10:57.408
one of my best friends in the office.

00:10:57.408 --> 00:10:58.929
He was the commander.

00:10:58.929 --> 00:11:01.365
And there were two others from
the class previous to mine.

00:11:01.365 --> 00:11:03.163
So I knew them all really well.

00:11:03.163 --> 00:11:06.058
I was actually taking my
son to a boy scout event

00:11:06.058 --> 00:11:08.803
and one of the parents
kind of took me to the side

00:11:08.803 --> 00:11:10.106
and said they had heard something.

00:11:10.106 --> 00:11:13.524
And then I called one of
my buddies who was at home

00:11:13.524 --> 00:11:16.219
and said, "Hey, turn on the
TV and see what's going on."

00:11:16.219 --> 00:11:20.069
And he said, he's like,
"We got to go to work."

00:11:20.069 --> 00:11:23.071
You know, I talked to my family about it.

00:11:23.071 --> 00:11:24.636
You know, we knew it was a risky business

00:11:24.636 --> 00:11:29.376
and that this didn't add
any new information really.

00:11:29.376 --> 00:11:32.154
It wasn't like a surprise
that this could happen.

00:11:32.154 --> 00:11:33.471
And in a weird way,

00:11:33.471 --> 00:11:36.288
it's kind of like, well,
you know, this happened

00:11:36.288 --> 00:11:40.049
and it's probably not going
to happen again for a while.

00:11:40.049 --> 00:11:41.205
We're going to have to be really careful

00:11:41.205 --> 00:11:43.510
and make sure it doesn't happen again.

00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:44.892
But I still wanted to go

00:11:44.892 --> 00:11:47.400
and I got the support
I needed from my family

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:48.753
to give it another try.

00:11:48.753 --> 00:11:51.143
I still have great confidence in NASA.

00:11:51.143 --> 00:11:53.053
Things are going to happen
every once in a while

00:11:53.053 --> 00:11:55.622
where the environment is going to win

00:11:55.622 --> 00:11:58.277
and you're going to have
a bad day, unfortunately.

00:11:58.277 --> 00:12:00.110
I think that all of us

00:12:01.047 --> 00:12:03.305
tried to think of everything
that could go wrong

00:12:03.305 --> 00:12:05.509
and try to have a solution for it.

00:12:05.509 --> 00:12:09.370
And everything that did go
wrong, we had a solution for.

00:12:09.370 --> 00:12:10.314
But there's always those things

00:12:10.314 --> 00:12:12.298
that you can't even imagine happening.

00:12:12.298 --> 00:12:13.327
And everything kind of happens,

00:12:13.327 --> 00:12:15.839
boom, boom, boom, boom, boom, on that day

00:12:15.839 --> 00:12:17.538
where, you know, there
may be 10 different things

00:12:17.538 --> 00:12:20.642
that happened that are unique,

00:12:20.642 --> 00:12:23.330
but they all happened at
the same time together.

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:26.753
And that's when you have a bad day.

00:12:26.753 --> 00:12:27.819
Things happen.

00:12:27.819 --> 00:12:28.908
You know, on the most recent mission,

00:12:28.908 --> 00:12:29.778
the Artemis II mission,

00:12:29.778 --> 00:12:32.169
there was talk about the heat shield.

00:12:32.169 --> 00:12:34.349
But they knew about that threat,

00:12:34.349 --> 00:12:37.562
and they studied and tested
and did everything they could.

00:12:37.562 --> 00:12:39.363
So I was pretty convinced

00:12:39.363 --> 00:12:41.833
that this is not going to be
a problem, the heat shield.

00:12:41.833 --> 00:12:42.717
They were going to have a problem.

00:12:42.717 --> 00:12:44.299
It's going to be something
no one thought of,

00:12:44.299 --> 00:12:46.180
and be like, "Ooh, we didn't
know that can happen."

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:49.378
I have high confidence
in the way they operate.

00:12:49.378 --> 00:12:53.484
I had the sense that on my
first flight, maybe foolishly,

00:12:53.484 --> 00:12:56.067
that the major danger was over,

00:12:57.689 --> 00:13:01.461
and we made it to orbit, which
was certainly in your face.

00:13:01.461 --> 00:13:03.043
This, you could get killed.

00:13:03.043 --> 00:13:04.908
And the space walks, we
had to be really careful.

00:13:04.908 --> 00:13:05.741
We talked about it,

00:13:05.741 --> 00:13:07.159
that we thought the second
most dangerous thing

00:13:07.159 --> 00:13:09.870
we were going to do was the space walks.

00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:12.466
And so not that we shouldn't have known

00:13:12.466 --> 00:13:16.077
that entry coming back could be dangerous.

00:13:16.077 --> 00:13:18.080
It just seemed like that was not...

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:19.934
We weren't going to
have a problem with it.

00:13:19.934 --> 00:13:22.159
I was convinced we were going to be OK.

00:13:22.159 --> 00:13:24.634
And so when we had the
accident, the Columbia accident,

00:13:24.634 --> 00:13:25.837
it was a reminder

00:13:25.837 --> 00:13:27.282
that, you know, you're not safe again

00:13:27.282 --> 00:13:28.569
until you're back on Earth.

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:35.966
The Hubble space walks were considered

00:13:35.966 --> 00:13:37.473
to be the most complex,

00:13:37.473 --> 00:13:39.678
meaning that there was so
many things you had to do.

00:13:39.678 --> 00:13:43.187
So it wasn't just built to be
a telescope and sent to space.

00:13:43.187 --> 00:13:45.520
It was built to be serviced.

00:13:46.392 --> 00:13:50.334
So just about everything
could be taken out of there.

00:13:50.334 --> 00:13:53.029
The solar arrays were in
clamps and with connectors

00:13:53.029 --> 00:13:55.229
so you could undo the
clamp, undo the connectors,

00:13:55.229 --> 00:13:57.220
remove the solar array, put a new one in.

00:13:57.220 --> 00:13:59.140
The Hubble Space Telescope

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:00.782
is about the size of a school bus,

00:14:00.782 --> 00:14:02.304
a standardized school bus.

00:14:02.304 --> 00:14:03.387
It's that size

00:14:03.387 --> 00:14:07.605
because it was made to be
as large as it could be

00:14:07.605 --> 00:14:09.927
and still fit in the payload
bay of the space shuttles.

00:14:09.927 --> 00:14:11.471
All of the instruments,

00:14:11.471 --> 00:14:12.952
some of them are the
size of a refrigerator,

00:14:12.952 --> 00:14:15.826
some of them the size
of a baby grand piano,

00:14:15.826 --> 00:14:19.893
all of those things could be
removed by undoing some latches

00:14:19.893 --> 00:14:21.703
and some connectors,

00:14:21.703 --> 00:14:22.902
removing it very carefully,

00:14:22.902 --> 00:14:25.202
putting a new one in, and
taking the old one home.

00:14:25.202 --> 00:14:27.010
And that was generally the drill.

00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:30.324
It was a dream of astronomers for years

00:14:30.324 --> 00:14:32.599
to get a telescope into space.

00:14:32.599 --> 00:14:35.485
Because even on the clearest
night at a very high altitude

00:14:35.485 --> 00:14:38.165
where the atmosphere is thin,

00:14:38.165 --> 00:14:40.212
you're still looking
through the atmosphere.

00:14:40.212 --> 00:14:42.493
When that starlight
comes in, it's distorted,

00:14:42.493 --> 00:14:44.011
and that's why stars twinkle,

00:14:44.011 --> 00:14:45.308
because it's distorting of the light.

00:14:45.308 --> 00:14:46.862
So "Twinkle, Twinkle Little Star"

00:14:46.862 --> 00:14:48.445
is written by someone on Earth,

00:14:48.445 --> 00:14:49.696
because when you're in space,

00:14:49.696 --> 00:14:50.529
you don't get that twinkling.

00:14:50.529 --> 00:14:51.840
There are perfect points of light.

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:53.395
Hubble's above our atmosphere

00:14:53.395 --> 00:14:55.984
and can look directly into the universe

00:14:55.984 --> 00:14:58.320
and is able to return
these beautiful images.

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:00.361
Telescope was launched back in 1990.

00:15:00.361 --> 00:15:01.926
We only go into Hubble a few times.

00:15:01.926 --> 00:15:04.410
Well, on space station,
there's always a crew there.

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:05.397
They could do a space walk

00:15:05.397 --> 00:15:06.457
if they need it, you know, tomorrow

00:15:06.457 --> 00:15:07.755
if something's going wrong,

00:15:07.755 --> 00:15:10.208
but there's no crew on Hubble's.

00:15:10.208 --> 00:15:12.947
You only can go out for a
certain amount of space walk.

00:15:12.947 --> 00:15:14.560
We did five on each one of my missions.

00:15:14.560 --> 00:15:17.039
We had two different teams trading off.

00:15:17.039 --> 00:15:18.687
We would get a lot of attention.

00:15:18.687 --> 00:15:21.324
"Hey, we need a new tool to
do this," and we would get it.

00:15:21.324 --> 00:15:22.344
Our pistol grip tool,

00:15:22.344 --> 00:15:24.349
this power tool we had was developed

00:15:24.349 --> 00:15:25.230
for the Hubble missions

00:15:25.230 --> 00:15:27.426
and it was used to also
build the space station.

00:15:27.426 --> 00:15:28.259
Helmet cameras.

00:15:28.259 --> 00:15:29.220
See all these helmet camera views you get

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:30.475
from these space walks?

00:15:30.475 --> 00:15:31.509
Well, that was a Hubble thing.

00:15:31.509 --> 00:15:33.222
They didn't have those things before

00:15:33.222 --> 00:15:34.997
because what they used to do on Hubble

00:15:34.997 --> 00:15:36.761
is when they would close the doors,

00:15:36.761 --> 00:15:38.097
when you would work inside
and close the doors,

00:15:38.097 --> 00:15:39.637
you had to do these closeout photos,

00:15:39.637 --> 00:15:42.304
which you physically have
a camera looking around,

00:15:42.304 --> 00:15:43.856
it took a long time.

00:15:43.856 --> 00:15:46.603
So what they did is they had the idea

00:15:46.603 --> 00:15:48.456
to put cameras on your head

00:15:48.456 --> 00:15:50.088
so that as you're doing the space walk,

00:15:50.088 --> 00:15:51.915
you know, they could get all
the information they needed

00:15:51.915 --> 00:15:53.695
about what it looked like inside of there.

00:15:53.695 --> 00:15:55.455
And that was then turned over as well

00:15:55.455 --> 00:15:58.791
to the other missions that
were not going to Hubble.

00:15:58.791 --> 00:16:03.064
So we had just about everything
we needed and wanted.

00:16:03.064 --> 00:16:05.480
And there were years in the making.

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:08.865
You wake up and the
spacewalkers have first dibs

00:16:08.865 --> 00:16:10.127
on the toilet that day

00:16:10.127 --> 00:16:11.123
because there's only one toilet.

00:16:11.123 --> 00:16:13.119
So you always want to
have a good breakfast

00:16:13.119 --> 00:16:15.722
because there's no food
inside of the space suit.

00:16:15.722 --> 00:16:16.610
The only nourishment you had,

00:16:16.610 --> 00:16:20.490
you had a 32 ounce bag of
water inside of there with you.

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:21.427
So that was it.

00:16:21.427 --> 00:16:22.635
And put your diaper on first.

00:16:22.635 --> 00:16:25.189
Then you put these
polypropylene underwear on

00:16:25.189 --> 00:16:26.412
to absorb your sweat.

00:16:26.412 --> 00:16:28.897
And you put some socks
on, keep your feet warm.

00:16:28.897 --> 00:16:32.007
And then you put a liquid
cooling garment on,

00:16:32.007 --> 00:16:33.532
which is like a fancy
pair of long underwear

00:16:33.532 --> 00:16:35.659
with lots of tubes on it.

00:16:35.659 --> 00:16:38.475
And so that's to bring the
cooling water over your body.

00:16:38.475 --> 00:16:41.653
You're typically more
concerned about getting too hot

00:16:41.653 --> 00:16:45.671
because you're in an enclosed
space suit that cannot leak

00:16:45.671 --> 00:16:46.814
or else you have trouble.

00:16:46.814 --> 00:16:50.738
So you have something to suck the air in

00:16:50.738 --> 00:16:53.497
to the life support system in your feet

00:16:53.497 --> 00:16:55.137
and in your arms.

00:16:55.137 --> 00:16:56.701
And that air will go in

00:16:56.701 --> 00:16:58.951
and get scrubbed for
CO2 and other particles.

00:16:58.951 --> 00:17:00.421
And then the fresh air is
going to come in over your head

00:17:00.421 --> 00:17:01.382
through the helmets.

00:17:01.382 --> 00:17:02.869
And then you start a pre-brief.

00:17:02.869 --> 00:17:03.783
So in our missions,

00:17:03.783 --> 00:17:07.074
we had about 40 minutes
of breathing 100% oxygen.

00:17:07.074 --> 00:17:10.277
You're going to go to a
lower pressure in the suit.

00:17:10.277 --> 00:17:12.238
We lowered the pressure in the vehicle.

00:17:12.238 --> 00:17:13.871
We could do this on the shuttle

00:17:13.871 --> 00:17:15.606
where you could lower the pressure

00:17:15.606 --> 00:17:18.623
from 14.71 atmosphere, like sea level,

00:17:18.623 --> 00:17:21.699
down to 10.2, which is like
being on a tall mountain.

00:17:21.699 --> 00:17:23.687
What that does is that helps rid the body

00:17:23.687 --> 00:17:25.559
of some of its nitrogen.

00:17:25.559 --> 00:17:28.416
So you don't want to get the
bends, decompression sickness.

00:17:28.416 --> 00:17:29.968
If you're in a higher pressure environment

00:17:29.968 --> 00:17:32.723
and then you immediately go to
a lower pressure environment,

00:17:32.723 --> 00:17:34.448
it could lead to some
neurological problems

00:17:34.448 --> 00:17:37.365
because the nitrogen in your system

00:17:38.348 --> 00:17:40.592
can cause trouble in the bloodstream

00:17:40.592 --> 00:17:42.032
and that's not good.

00:17:42.032 --> 00:17:44.153
There's a hatch that leads
to space, which is locked

00:17:44.153 --> 00:17:45.379
or else you have a big hole in the space

00:17:45.379 --> 00:17:46.365
if you know that.

00:17:46.365 --> 00:17:48.896
But there's the inner hatch
that's open up to that point

00:17:48.896 --> 00:17:50.740
where, you know, you got
in there and everything.

00:17:50.740 --> 00:17:52.329
And then, you know,
everyone says, "Adios,"

00:17:52.329 --> 00:17:54.822
and then they close the
inner hatch and lock it.

00:17:54.822 --> 00:17:57.477
So now they're getting rid
of all the air in the airlock

00:17:57.477 --> 00:17:59.268
until it goes down to vacuum.

00:17:59.268 --> 00:18:01.032
Those moments where you're in there

00:18:01.032 --> 00:18:02.585
with your space walking buddy,

00:18:02.585 --> 00:18:04.681
kind of looking at each
other a little bit,

00:18:04.681 --> 00:18:06.202
just kind waiting to go outside,

00:18:06.202 --> 00:18:07.565
thinking about maybe your first thing

00:18:07.565 --> 00:18:08.890
you're going to do, whatever.

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:11.802
And I remember my first
time looking at that hatch.

00:18:11.802 --> 00:18:14.568
And even though I'd been
in space for a couple days,

00:18:14.568 --> 00:18:16.352
I felt there was a really big difference

00:18:16.352 --> 00:18:18.013
between being inside of a spaceship

00:18:18.013 --> 00:18:19.897
and going outside of it.

00:18:19.897 --> 00:18:23.716
You know, like that's
space out there, man.

00:18:23.716 --> 00:18:26.000
The only thing that's
protecting me is my spacesuit.

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:27.540
And it was like this door, you know?

00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:29.544
That's the door to space.

00:18:29.544 --> 00:18:31.758
Typically, it's an experienced
person and a new person.

00:18:31.758 --> 00:18:33.296
So on my first flight was
a guy named Jim Newman

00:18:33.296 --> 00:18:35.620
who had space walk many times
before and other missions.

00:18:35.620 --> 00:18:38.042
So he went out first and made
sure the coast was cleared,

00:18:38.042 --> 00:18:40.475
got our tethers set up and
he got everything ready.

00:18:40.475 --> 00:18:42.467
The telescope is not
that far away from you.

00:18:42.467 --> 00:18:43.908
It's out in the payload bay

00:18:43.908 --> 00:18:46.904
and about maybe 50 or
60 feet away from you.

00:18:46.904 --> 00:18:49.032
So it's not that far
away from the airlock.

00:18:49.032 --> 00:18:51.117
Once the airlock goes to vacuum,

00:18:51.117 --> 00:18:51.950
the only thing you're hearing

00:18:51.950 --> 00:18:54.308
is what's coming over your com cap.

00:18:54.308 --> 00:18:55.584
It's kind of peaceful.

00:18:55.584 --> 00:18:58.005
And you hear in the background,
you hear like this hum.

00:18:58.005 --> 00:19:00.826
It's the air pump water separator.

00:19:00.826 --> 00:19:02.885
It's like a little fan that goes

00:19:02.885 --> 00:19:05.669
and it keeps the cooling pump going

00:19:05.669 --> 00:19:07.529
and the ventilation going.

00:19:07.529 --> 00:19:09.500
This miraculous small little pump.

00:19:09.500 --> 00:19:11.544
There's one spacewalker's
on the robot arm,

00:19:11.544 --> 00:19:13.815
and that means the robot
arm operator's going to move

00:19:13.815 --> 00:19:15.660
the spacewalker around.

00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:18.166
The other person is what
we call the free floater

00:19:18.166 --> 00:19:19.350
and they're moving
around with their hands,

00:19:19.350 --> 00:19:20.677
moving around this way,

00:19:20.677 --> 00:19:21.640
and they can climb all over the place.

00:19:21.640 --> 00:19:23.982
You have a safety tether
attached to the arm

00:19:23.982 --> 00:19:25.121
in case you start floating away.

00:19:25.121 --> 00:19:26.072
But that's not going to happen.

00:19:26.072 --> 00:19:27.001
So the way to get out to the telescope

00:19:27.001 --> 00:19:28.678
is you go along the side
of the space shuttle.

00:19:28.678 --> 00:19:30.557
There's handrails that get you out there.

00:19:30.557 --> 00:19:31.390
And then he said, "All right, Mike,

00:19:31.390 --> 00:19:32.223
you're clear to come out."

00:19:32.223 --> 00:19:34.249
And he stuck my head out of the airlock

00:19:34.249 --> 00:19:37.313
and he's up on a handrail on the bulkhead,

00:19:37.313 --> 00:19:39.765
the forward bulkhead of the
shuttle looking down at me.

00:19:39.765 --> 00:19:42.331
He had his visor up so
I could see his face.

00:19:42.331 --> 00:19:44.849
And it was, you know, he's
got this big smile on his face

00:19:44.849 --> 00:19:47.292
and above his head is Africa.

00:19:47.292 --> 00:19:49.567
So the thought that went
through my mind was,

00:19:49.567 --> 00:19:52.282
"Whoa, how am I going to
get anything done out here?"

00:19:52.282 --> 00:19:53.115
In front of me,

00:19:53.115 --> 00:19:54.256
there was a handrail for the space shuttle

00:19:54.256 --> 00:19:56.576
and the curved handrail above the airlock.

00:19:56.576 --> 00:19:58.008
And there was a foot restraint here,

00:19:58.008 --> 00:19:59.796
just like there wasn't a training pool.

00:19:59.796 --> 00:20:01.789
There was a vent duct coming on each side.

00:20:01.789 --> 00:20:02.760
And I was like, "I know what I'm doing.

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:05.593
I'm going to rely on my training here

00:20:06.456 --> 00:20:07.908
and start off that way."

00:20:07.908 --> 00:20:08.759
I mean, you're out in space.

00:20:08.759 --> 00:20:09.958
You know, you see the planet,

00:20:09.958 --> 00:20:12.021
and it's just unbelievable.

00:20:12.021 --> 00:20:13.183
I remember the first time,

00:20:13.183 --> 00:20:16.592
one of my first tasks the
first time I was out there,

00:20:16.592 --> 00:20:18.174
I was got into the robot arm

00:20:18.174 --> 00:20:20.302
and went up high on the telescope.

00:20:20.302 --> 00:20:22.003
And so I was getting, you know,
taken up on the telescope,

00:20:22.003 --> 00:20:23.532
just riding along.

00:20:23.532 --> 00:20:26.657
And I could see down
then as I got up high.

00:20:26.657 --> 00:20:28.628
I could see down over
the wing of the shuttle

00:20:28.628 --> 00:20:30.430
and I could see the NASA meatball

00:20:30.430 --> 00:20:33.900
and Space Shuttle Columbia
name and the American flag

00:20:33.900 --> 00:20:36.735
and looking in the planets
out in the distance.

00:20:36.735 --> 00:20:40.464
And the first space walk I was
on kind of went really well.

00:20:40.464 --> 00:20:42.875
Everything was working
and we were humming along

00:20:42.875 --> 00:20:44.857
and sticking to business.

00:20:44.857 --> 00:20:46.858
Everything was working,
going according to plan.

00:20:46.858 --> 00:20:48.561
We actually got a little bit ahead.

00:20:48.561 --> 00:20:51.419
We finished everything we were
supposed to a little early.

00:20:51.419 --> 00:20:52.904
You feel pretty, pretty drained.

00:20:52.904 --> 00:20:54.083
It's a mental exercise too.

00:20:54.083 --> 00:20:55.660
You're out there.

00:20:55.660 --> 00:20:58.137
You plan the space walk to be
about six and a half hours.

00:20:58.137 --> 00:21:01.251
And then the ones I had, I
think were all longer than that,

00:21:01.251 --> 00:21:03.471
like more like around seven and change.

00:21:03.471 --> 00:21:05.305
And it's pretty intense.

00:21:05.305 --> 00:21:08.105
Physically, I was in the
best shape of my life

00:21:08.105 --> 00:21:11.061
before each one of my missions.

00:21:11.061 --> 00:21:15.464
So I was pretty well prepared
physically to do these things.

00:21:15.464 --> 00:21:16.783
But still, it's pretty draining.

00:21:16.783 --> 00:21:19.382
I mean you're pretty beat

00:21:19.382 --> 00:21:22.799
when you get back in from your space walk.

00:21:23.749 --> 00:21:24.872
And you get out of there.

00:21:24.872 --> 00:21:27.798
You know, you're ready to clean up

00:21:27.798 --> 00:21:29.758
and get something to eat.

00:21:34.880 --> 00:21:35.987
- [Interviewer] What's the protocol

00:21:35.987 --> 00:21:38.570
if an astronaut starts floating
away from the space shuttle?

00:21:38.570 --> 00:21:40.897
- There is a protocol if
you start flowing away.

00:21:40.897 --> 00:21:42.383
You always have to have
your safety tether hooked up

00:21:42.383 --> 00:21:44.431
just in case somehow you
came off the structure

00:21:44.431 --> 00:21:46.834
or came out of the robot
arm or something like that.

00:21:46.834 --> 00:21:49.408
That safety tether will take care of you.

00:21:49.408 --> 00:21:51.908
It's a titanium piece of cord.

00:21:53.249 --> 00:21:56.064
If that came undone,
then you're a free flyer,

00:21:56.064 --> 00:21:57.294
then you're out of luck.

00:21:57.294 --> 00:21:59.646
And that's never happened.

00:21:59.646 --> 00:22:00.722
But if it did,

00:22:00.722 --> 00:22:01.749
on the space station,

00:22:01.749 --> 00:22:04.190
you have a little jet pack you could fly,

00:22:04.190 --> 00:22:05.823
that you could fly back to the...

00:22:05.823 --> 00:22:08.865
Because they can't come
get you at space station.

00:22:08.865 --> 00:22:10.750
You can't undock a
vehicle to come get you.

00:22:10.750 --> 00:22:11.660
It's unlikely anyway.

00:22:11.660 --> 00:22:12.691
But if you came undone,

00:22:12.691 --> 00:22:15.004
you would fly yourself,
you had a little jet pack.

00:22:15.004 --> 00:22:16.532
But we didn't have that on Hubble.

00:22:16.532 --> 00:22:17.931
But if that happened,
you start floating away,

00:22:17.931 --> 00:22:21.053
become a free flyer,
and you can't get back.

00:22:21.053 --> 00:22:23.113
The commander would then come and get you.

00:22:23.113 --> 00:22:25.771
Since we were space
walking on the shuttle,

00:22:25.771 --> 00:22:27.750
you could still fly the shuttle around.

00:22:27.750 --> 00:22:29.118
He would take over the
controls of the shuttle

00:22:29.118 --> 00:22:31.402
and fly it to you so you would grab it.

00:22:31.402 --> 00:22:35.543
We called it the lost
crew person procedure.

00:22:35.543 --> 00:22:37.451
And we would brief it
just to go through it.

00:22:37.451 --> 00:22:38.399
Because it's like an emer-

00:22:38.399 --> 00:22:40.040
Lost crew person is what you do.

00:22:40.040 --> 00:22:42.134
But my friend Rick
Linnehan on my first flight

00:22:42.134 --> 00:22:44.462
had a bit of a dark sense of humor, maybe.

00:22:44.462 --> 00:22:46.316
You're in space, and he's
briefing us for the first time.

00:22:46.316 --> 00:22:47.544
All these saying you know,

00:22:47.544 --> 00:22:48.783
"You tether this," and blah blah blah.

00:22:48.783 --> 00:22:50.834
And he goes, "Lost crewperson.

00:22:50.834 --> 00:22:53.049
Don't worry about it.
We got two more inside."

00:22:57.500 --> 00:23:00.058
So the second mission to Hubble,

00:23:00.058 --> 00:23:02.106
what ended up being the final mission,

00:23:02.106 --> 00:23:03.902
we knew it was going to
be the final mission,

00:23:03.902 --> 00:23:06.061
and no one's been there since,

00:23:06.061 --> 00:23:08.311
and we were trying to
do as much as we could.

00:23:08.311 --> 00:23:12.394
Second mission, STS-125
was in 2009, May of 2009.

00:23:13.584 --> 00:23:17.024
There were two instruments
that had power failures,

00:23:17.024 --> 00:23:20.274
and they couldn't turn these things on,

00:23:21.215 --> 00:23:22.954
but you know, they were great instruments,

00:23:22.954 --> 00:23:26.480
and we did not have replacements for them.

00:23:26.480 --> 00:23:27.347
One of these two

00:23:27.347 --> 00:23:30.320
is called the Space Telescope
Imaging Spectrograph

00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:32.213
that could do things like
analyze the atmospheres

00:23:32.213 --> 00:23:34.020
of far of planets.

00:23:34.020 --> 00:23:36.398
And they wanted to bring
this capability back.

00:23:36.398 --> 00:23:38.766
Now these things were put
together over years in time

00:23:38.766 --> 00:23:41.783
by really conscientious
engineers and technicians.

00:23:41.783 --> 00:23:42.842
Their goal was to make it

00:23:42.842 --> 00:23:44.820
so no one could ever
take this apart again.

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:46.242
It was buttoned up

00:23:46.242 --> 00:23:50.113
so that there would be no
danger to it during launch.

00:23:50.113 --> 00:23:52.458
It could withstand launch
and the rigors of space

00:23:52.458 --> 00:23:54.437
and no one was ever going
to open these things again.

00:23:54.437 --> 00:23:56.966
This panel had 111 small screws,

00:23:56.966 --> 00:23:58.820
each of which had a washer.

00:23:58.820 --> 00:24:00.284
And just to make sure the
screws never back out,

00:24:00.284 --> 00:24:01.785
they put glue on the threads.

00:24:01.785 --> 00:24:05.130
But that task alone, over
100 new tools were designed.

00:24:05.130 --> 00:24:07.353
So I was the lead spacewalker

00:24:07.353 --> 00:24:10.125
and had a fellow out there
with me, was a new guy.

00:24:10.125 --> 00:24:13.289
Mike Good, my good buddy
and a great spacewalker.

00:24:13.289 --> 00:24:15.227
So we practice and practice,

00:24:15.227 --> 00:24:18.151
and we practiced working
together of every little step

00:24:18.151 --> 00:24:21.873
of everything we were going to
do to repair the instrument.

00:24:21.873 --> 00:24:24.652
You were doing this
really delicate procedure

00:24:24.652 --> 00:24:26.394
with these gloves on,

00:24:26.394 --> 00:24:29.702
and the tools are designed to make it

00:24:29.702 --> 00:24:31.259
so the gloves will work with them.

00:24:31.259 --> 00:24:34.009
So if you had, like, a regular tool

00:24:34.910 --> 00:24:37.684
that you might be familiar
with, like a crescent wrench,

00:24:37.684 --> 00:24:41.245
that thing will have an extra
added handle piece to it.

00:24:41.245 --> 00:24:44.605
Some special space rubber stuff,

00:24:44.605 --> 00:24:48.522
hard material that'll
allow you to use the tool

00:24:49.864 --> 00:24:51.720
without having to have a death grip on it

00:24:51.720 --> 00:24:53.039
like you would on Earth.

00:24:53.039 --> 00:24:55.089
I felt you're, like,
trying to work on your car,

00:24:55.089 --> 00:24:57.306
like change the oil in the
car, with boxing gloves.

00:24:57.306 --> 00:24:59.850
It was the most ambitious
space walk we've ever tried.

00:24:59.850 --> 00:25:01.368
Capture plate on there.

00:25:01.368 --> 00:25:02.475
Just gets all these little screws.

00:25:02.475 --> 00:25:05.261
And to do that, we had
to remove four screws

00:25:05.261 --> 00:25:07.685
on the outer side of this.

00:25:07.685 --> 00:25:09.654
The easiest thing I was
going to do that day

00:25:09.654 --> 00:25:10.898
was remove a handrail

00:25:10.898 --> 00:25:13.627
that was used to insert the
instrument years earlier.

00:25:13.627 --> 00:25:16.062
That handrail had two
big screws at the top,

00:25:16.062 --> 00:25:17.800
two big hex screws at the top,

00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:20.540
and two big hex screws at the bottom.

00:25:20.540 --> 00:25:21.880
So we weren't worried about those screws.

00:25:21.880 --> 00:25:22.812
They were beefy.

00:25:22.812 --> 00:25:24.233
And we even used the old power tool.

00:25:24.233 --> 00:25:25.940
We didn't even need any fancy stuff,

00:25:25.940 --> 00:25:27.671
and it was just going to
take, like, 30 seconds.

00:25:27.671 --> 00:25:29.163
There's one line in the checklist.

00:25:29.163 --> 00:25:31.689
It was so simple. We had
no recourse for this.

00:25:31.689 --> 00:25:33.306
Like, even I couldn't mess it up.

00:25:33.306 --> 00:25:35.436
The one at the bottom left
comes loose, you know?

00:25:35.436 --> 00:25:36.421
I could feel that.

00:25:36.421 --> 00:25:38.950
The one at the bottom right,
it's just spinning my tool.

00:25:38.950 --> 00:25:40.787
And I was like, "What's going on?"

00:25:40.787 --> 00:25:42.851
And I removed the tool, and
I look with my helmet light,

00:25:42.851 --> 00:25:45.936
and I bend down and I look,
and it's no longer a hex head.

00:25:45.936 --> 00:25:48.251
It's gnarled that piece of metal.

00:25:48.251 --> 00:25:49.713
And I realized, holy cow.

00:25:49.713 --> 00:25:50.603
That screw's never coming out.

00:25:50.603 --> 00:25:51.836
Handrail's not coming off.

00:25:51.836 --> 00:25:53.460
111 screws aren't coming out.

00:25:53.460 --> 00:25:56.164
We'll never replace the power supply.

00:25:56.164 --> 00:25:58.508
We're not going to bring
the instrument back to life.

00:25:58.508 --> 00:26:00.952
We'll never find out if
there's life in the universe.

00:26:00.952 --> 00:26:03.083
And everyone's going to blame me.

00:26:03.083 --> 00:26:06.158
That wasn't far from the
situation at that moment.

00:26:06.158 --> 00:26:07.011
Before I said anything,

00:26:07.011 --> 00:26:08.508
I just kind of leaned
out of the telescope,

00:26:08.508 --> 00:26:09.567
because I was working in there,

00:26:09.567 --> 00:26:12.313
and I leaned out, and we
were over the Pacific Ocean,

00:26:12.313 --> 00:26:13.627
and I thought to myself,

00:26:13.627 --> 00:26:16.164
I can't even get to a
hardware store if I wanted to.

00:26:16.164 --> 00:26:17.690
Like, I've really messed up this time.

00:26:17.690 --> 00:26:19.452
I don't know what we're going to do.

00:26:19.452 --> 00:26:21.998
And for about an hour or
so, we were troubleshooting,

00:26:21.998 --> 00:26:24.296
and I didn't have any good idea.

00:26:24.296 --> 00:26:26.348
I mean, I was trying to think,
what else could we remove?

00:26:26.348 --> 00:26:28.393
What other tools could we try?

00:26:28.393 --> 00:26:29.485
Could we get it from the back?

00:26:29.485 --> 00:26:31.197
And oh, I knew it was bad,

00:26:31.197 --> 00:26:33.891
because I knew the repair
backwards and forwards,

00:26:33.891 --> 00:26:37.629
and I knew that there's
no way to get around this.

00:26:37.629 --> 00:26:40.651
But we had a clever
engineer named Jim Corbo.

00:26:40.651 --> 00:26:41.484
He thought to himself,

00:26:41.484 --> 00:26:44.030
what would he do if he was in his garage?

00:26:44.030 --> 00:26:45.161
What would you do at home?

00:26:45.161 --> 00:26:47.745
He said, "Sometimes brute
force is the answer."

00:26:47.745 --> 00:26:49.094
He would just try to rip that thing off,

00:26:49.094 --> 00:26:50.136
because it was loose at the top, you know?

00:26:50.136 --> 00:26:52.035
You'd just yank it and break it.

00:26:52.035 --> 00:26:54.381
And he called over to the
Goddard Space Flight Center,

00:26:54.381 --> 00:26:57.824
and they're standing by, you
know, watching this in horror.

00:26:57.824 --> 00:27:01.241
That years of, hours of training for this

00:27:03.382 --> 00:27:05.818
and all the aerial development tools

00:27:05.818 --> 00:27:07.915
have just gone up in smoke.

00:27:07.915 --> 00:27:10.115
They were watching, and
Jim told them their idea

00:27:10.115 --> 00:27:12.296
and they came up with was the idea

00:27:12.296 --> 00:27:14.106
to pull an instrument out of a clean room,

00:27:14.106 --> 00:27:16.820
put it in the same
configuration with the handrail,

00:27:16.820 --> 00:27:19.650
and they pulled on the top of
the handrail with a fish scale

00:27:19.650 --> 00:27:21.349
to see how hard it would
be to break that bolt

00:27:21.349 --> 00:27:23.688
at the bottom that was still there.

00:27:23.688 --> 00:27:26.229
And it broke that bolt
at 60 pounds of force.

00:27:26.229 --> 00:27:27.321
Tony told me afterwards,

00:27:27.321 --> 00:27:29.146
he was worried about breaking that

00:27:29.146 --> 00:27:32.045
and debris going flying and
hitting, getting me or Mike.

00:27:32.045 --> 00:27:34.626
You break stuff in space,
you're asking for trouble.

00:27:34.626 --> 00:27:37.000
Shrapnel or sharp edge
or something like that.

00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:39.281
If you have a projectile coming flying

00:27:39.281 --> 00:27:41.343
because you just broke a piece of metal

00:27:41.343 --> 00:27:43.633
and it hit my spacesuit
or bueno spacesuit,

00:27:43.633 --> 00:27:45.121
it could cause a real problem.

00:27:45.121 --> 00:27:46.543
The technique they wanted us to use,

00:27:46.543 --> 00:27:47.768
which was not to just yank it,

00:27:47.768 --> 00:27:49.160
but to kind of break,

00:27:49.160 --> 00:27:51.857
like kind of just not yank
the whole thing off at one,

00:27:51.857 --> 00:27:53.090
but try to like weaken it,

00:27:53.090 --> 00:27:54.292
you know, kind of yield it a little bit

00:27:54.292 --> 00:27:56.048
and then in a couple steps get it

00:27:56.048 --> 00:27:58.179
so it just need a little tug at the end.

00:27:58.179 --> 00:28:00.050
It all went perfectly after that.

00:28:00.050 --> 00:28:01.135
So this space walk went pretty long.

00:28:01.135 --> 00:28:03.510
It was over eight hours,
but we got it all done,

00:28:03.510 --> 00:28:05.637
and they brought the
instrument back to life,

00:28:05.637 --> 00:28:07.450
and I was just so happy.

00:28:07.450 --> 00:28:08.977
I remember we went back to the airlock

00:28:08.977 --> 00:28:11.297
and Scooter, Scott Altman, my commander,

00:28:11.297 --> 00:28:12.805
was my very good friend,

00:28:12.805 --> 00:28:13.960
comes over the communication line

00:28:13.960 --> 00:28:17.053
and he says, "Mass, what are you doing?"

00:28:17.053 --> 00:28:18.427
And I was like, "Oh, I'm just, you know,

00:28:18.427 --> 00:28:20.351
cleaning up the airlock a little bit."

00:28:20.351 --> 00:28:23.402
And he goes, "Is there
anything you're doing now

00:28:23.402 --> 00:28:25.709
that you can't do later?"

00:28:25.709 --> 00:28:27.179
And he goes, "Why don't
you just go outside

00:28:27.179 --> 00:28:28.756
and enjoy the view?"

00:28:28.756 --> 00:28:32.570
After this, you know, psychic trauma

00:28:32.570 --> 00:28:35.761
that I went through breaking
that bolt, stripping that bolt,

00:28:35.761 --> 00:28:38.893
you know, so I was just
able to really relax,

00:28:38.893 --> 00:28:42.678
and I just let go and took in the view.

00:28:42.678 --> 00:28:44.972
And it was a day pass, it was daytime.

00:28:44.972 --> 00:28:48.316
We were over to Pacific
kind of heading over Hawaii

00:28:48.316 --> 00:28:51.790
and it was just magnificent
seeing the ocean and the clouds.

00:28:51.790 --> 00:28:55.040
If we didn't get that handrail removed,

00:28:56.486 --> 00:28:58.644
we would've not brought
that instrument back.

00:28:58.644 --> 00:29:00.007
And it's still working today.

00:29:04.840 --> 00:29:06.169
NASA approached me and said,

00:29:06.169 --> 00:29:09.897
"We have this idea to
send a tweet from space."

00:29:09.897 --> 00:29:12.390
They asked my commander
about it and he said, "OK."

00:29:12.390 --> 00:29:13.981
And they thought I was
the right guy to do it

00:29:13.981 --> 00:29:14.814
for whatever reason.

00:29:14.814 --> 00:29:15.685
I was like, "OK."

00:29:15.685 --> 00:29:17.051
But I didn't have a Twitter account.

00:29:17.051 --> 00:29:18.387
I wasn't paying attention
to anything really about

00:29:18.387 --> 00:29:19.938
except the mission at that time.

00:29:19.938 --> 00:29:21.892
First tweet from space,

00:29:21.892 --> 00:29:23.952
I didn't think about it very much.

00:29:23.952 --> 00:29:26.578
I just kind of, whatever came to mind.

00:29:26.578 --> 00:29:27.501
I didn't think. I didn't know.

00:29:27.501 --> 00:29:28.866
I should have thought
about it some more maybe,

00:29:28.866 --> 00:29:31.660
but I wrote, "Launch was awesome.

00:29:31.660 --> 00:29:33.161
The adventure of a lifetime has begun."

00:29:33.161 --> 00:29:34.485
Something like that.

00:29:34.485 --> 00:29:35.426
And that was on a Monday.

00:29:35.426 --> 00:29:37.240
They made fun of me on
"Saturday Night Live."

00:29:37.240 --> 00:29:39.116
Seth Myers was doing weekend update

00:29:39.116 --> 00:29:42.145
and he said, "We have the first
treat from space and it is:

00:29:42.145 --> 00:29:43.846
'Launch was awesome.'

00:29:43.846 --> 00:29:46.945
In 40 years, we've gone from,
'One giant leap for mankind,'

00:29:46.945 --> 00:29:49.337
to, 'Launch was awesome.'"

00:29:49.337 --> 00:29:51.038
And then he went on to say,

00:29:51.038 --> 00:29:53.647
"If we ever encounter
life in the universe,

00:29:53.647 --> 00:29:55.517
I assume this is how I'll be notified."

00:29:55.517 --> 00:29:56.630
And it shows my Twitter thing that says,

00:29:56.630 --> 00:29:58.271
"Geez, dudes. Aliens."

00:29:58.271 --> 00:29:59.194
So I got made fun of.

00:29:59.194 --> 00:30:01.076
My kids' thought it was cool though.

00:30:01.076 --> 00:30:03.439
The first one was a
little, maybe not the best,

00:30:03.439 --> 00:30:05.327
but I think they got better.

00:30:05.327 --> 00:30:06.967
After my first mission,

00:30:06.967 --> 00:30:11.210
the space station
developed the possibility,

00:30:11.210 --> 00:30:15.020
the capability of a phone
call that you could make.

00:30:15.020 --> 00:30:18.103
There was an internet protocol phone.

00:30:19.790 --> 00:30:21.592
You can't call the space station,

00:30:21.592 --> 00:30:23.507
but the space station could call you.

00:30:23.507 --> 00:30:25.982
If you ever get a phone call, folks,

00:30:25.982 --> 00:30:28.413
and it's 281 with a 244
exchange, pick it up.

00:30:28.413 --> 00:30:30.157
It might be the space station calling.

00:30:30.157 --> 00:30:31.853
'Because if you don't pick it
up and they leave a message

00:30:31.853 --> 00:30:32.724
like, "Ah man."

00:30:32.724 --> 00:30:33.999
Then you got, "Hey, email me."

00:30:33.999 --> 00:30:34.832
You know, they go to email.

00:30:34.832 --> 00:30:36.159
"Call me again, will you?"

00:30:36.159 --> 00:30:37.814
I was like, "I don't know if
this is such a good idea,"

00:30:37.814 --> 00:30:38.911
because we're really busy.

00:30:38.911 --> 00:30:40.025
You know, the missions,

00:30:40.025 --> 00:30:41.000
we don't have a lot of free time.

00:30:41.000 --> 00:30:42.798
The space station you
get like some time off

00:30:42.798 --> 00:30:44.278
because you're up there,
you're living up there.

00:30:44.278 --> 00:30:46.804
So our families knew we had this.

00:30:46.804 --> 00:30:47.637
We never used it.

00:30:47.637 --> 00:30:48.567
We didn't even set the thing up.

00:30:48.567 --> 00:30:51.059
So then when we got back from
my second fight, you know,

00:30:51.059 --> 00:30:53.576
our family was like, "Glad you're back,

00:30:53.576 --> 00:30:54.968
but why didn't you call?"

00:30:54.968 --> 00:30:56.568
And we set up the expectation we had.

00:30:56.568 --> 00:30:58.538
I knew that was a bad idea.

00:31:03.350 --> 00:31:06.172
The landing day's pretty exciting.

00:31:06.172 --> 00:31:08.125
It's kind of bittersweet because
you know you're coming home

00:31:08.125 --> 00:31:10.828
and you know, you've had a good mission,

00:31:10.828 --> 00:31:12.295
but you're also excited about coming home.

00:31:12.295 --> 00:31:14.450
So that's a good part of it.

00:31:14.450 --> 00:31:15.934
And you get ready that day.

00:31:15.934 --> 00:31:17.301
It's all about, you know,

00:31:17.301 --> 00:31:18.952
preparing the space shuttle to come back

00:31:18.952 --> 00:31:20.494
and get your space suits on,

00:31:20.494 --> 00:31:23.082
get everything set up,
and go over the briefings.

00:31:23.082 --> 00:31:26.973
My first mission, this was
before the Columbia accident.

00:31:26.973 --> 00:31:28.513
I know we were moving along

00:31:28.513 --> 00:31:29.722
and going through the checklist,

00:31:29.722 --> 00:31:34.660
but there wasn't the same
sense of urgency or intensity.

00:31:34.660 --> 00:31:37.393
After we had the accident
on entry with Columbia,

00:31:37.393 --> 00:31:38.851
then my second flight, which was after,

00:31:38.851 --> 00:31:41.175
there was a little more
intense feeling, I think,

00:31:41.175 --> 00:31:42.972
about what we were facing.

00:31:42.972 --> 00:31:44.306
And we should have had that same intensity

00:31:44.306 --> 00:31:45.166
on the first flight,

00:31:45.166 --> 00:31:46.920
but for me, it wasn't there.

00:31:46.920 --> 00:31:51.288
We were coming in on kind
of a sloping kind of path

00:31:51.288 --> 00:31:53.213
across the planet to come in.

00:31:53.213 --> 00:31:54.984
So it was pretty smooth.

00:31:54.984 --> 00:31:56.134
And you're landing on a runway,

00:31:56.134 --> 00:31:58.250
so that was pretty smooth as well.

00:31:58.250 --> 00:32:00.668
My first flight we landed
at the Kennedy Space Center,

00:32:00.668 --> 00:32:01.547
which is the preferred place

00:32:01.547 --> 00:32:02.525
because that's where
you're going to take off.

00:32:02.525 --> 00:32:04.534
It was going to leave from the next time.

00:32:04.534 --> 00:32:05.499
My second flight though,

00:32:05.499 --> 00:32:07.154
we had bad weather at
the Kennedy Space Center

00:32:07.154 --> 00:32:10.088
so we got a couple extra
days in orbit, which was fun.

00:32:10.088 --> 00:32:11.493
But then they eventually
had to bring us home

00:32:11.493 --> 00:32:13.603
and it was still bad weather
at Kennedy Space Center.

00:32:13.603 --> 00:32:15.029
So that second flight,

00:32:15.029 --> 00:32:17.856
we landed at Edwards Air
Force Base in California.

00:32:17.856 --> 00:32:19.063
Weather's always good there.

00:32:19.063 --> 00:32:20.834
There were really only
three approved runways.

00:32:20.834 --> 00:32:24.707
We had some emergency landing
sites coming up the East Coast

00:32:24.707 --> 00:32:26.122
and over in other places.

00:32:26.122 --> 00:32:28.598
If you couldn't make it to space

00:32:28.598 --> 00:32:30.103
and you can make it across the Atlantic,

00:32:30.103 --> 00:32:32.078
in Southern Europe and in Northern Africa,

00:32:32.078 --> 00:32:34.441
there were landing sites as well.

00:32:34.441 --> 00:32:36.370
But the primary landing sites,

00:32:36.370 --> 00:32:38.997
the primary one was the
Kennedy Space Center.

00:32:38.997 --> 00:32:41.706
The second one was Edwards Air Force Base.

00:32:41.706 --> 00:32:44.634
The third one was White
Sand's Missile Range.

00:32:44.634 --> 00:32:46.137
You're going to feel a little off balance

00:32:46.137 --> 00:32:48.366
because now your vestibular
system is kicking in again

00:32:48.366 --> 00:32:51.844
after being silent for
the past two weeks or so.

00:32:51.844 --> 00:32:53.438
So just feeling a little off balance.

00:32:53.438 --> 00:32:55.062
You know, you kind of
walk around with your legs

00:32:55.062 --> 00:32:58.235
like Frankenstein sort of, you know,

00:32:58.235 --> 00:32:59.877
kind of walking around there

00:32:59.877 --> 00:33:03.342
with leg spread apart to keep
your balance as best you can

00:33:03.342 --> 00:33:04.926
and just kind of getting used

00:33:04.926 --> 00:33:06.816
to being back on the planet again.

00:33:06.816 --> 00:33:08.968
You got to be careful your first waking up

00:33:08.968 --> 00:33:10.567
your first day back on the planet,

00:33:10.567 --> 00:33:11.784
that you don't just float out of bed

00:33:11.784 --> 00:33:13.380
or try to float out of bed.

00:33:13.380 --> 00:33:14.683
I dropped the bag of groceries

00:33:14.683 --> 00:33:17.499
in a second day back in the driveway.

00:33:17.499 --> 00:33:19.501
Kind of purposely put it up here behind me

00:33:19.501 --> 00:33:21.918
and forgot where I was, yeah.

00:33:27.000 --> 00:33:30.263
I dreamt about being an astronaut
when I was six years old

00:33:30.263 --> 00:33:32.051
and I saw Neil Armstrong on the moon.

00:33:32.051 --> 00:33:33.815
I wanted to grow up to be just like him.

00:33:33.815 --> 00:33:34.689
He was my hero.

00:33:34.689 --> 00:33:36.846
But then I found out I
was afraid of heights,

00:33:36.846 --> 00:33:39.012
and trying to grow up
to be like your hero,

00:33:39.012 --> 00:33:39.885
like how do you do that?

00:33:39.885 --> 00:33:40.945
That's impossible.

00:33:40.945 --> 00:33:42.557
So when I was a senior in college,

00:33:42.557 --> 00:33:43.390
I went to the movies

00:33:43.390 --> 00:33:45.415
and saw this movie, "The Right Stuff,"

00:33:45.415 --> 00:33:46.976
which is about the
original seven astronauts

00:33:46.976 --> 00:33:49.180
and the test pilots that came before them.

00:33:49.180 --> 00:33:51.547
And it made me want to grow
up to be an astronaut again.

00:33:51.547 --> 00:33:53.019
The hardest thing about being an astronaut

00:33:53.019 --> 00:33:55.094
is getting the opportunity,

00:33:55.094 --> 00:33:57.274
because so many qualified
people are trying to get it,

00:33:57.274 --> 00:33:58.503
and it's not easy.

00:33:58.503 --> 00:34:02.406
And it's kind of out of your
control to a certain extent.

00:34:02.406 --> 00:34:04.387
I applied for the class of 1990

00:34:04.387 --> 00:34:07.403
and got rejected outright
for the class of '92

00:34:07.403 --> 00:34:08.795
and got rejected outright.

00:34:08.795 --> 00:34:11.851
The class of '94, which ended
up being the class of '95,

00:34:11.851 --> 00:34:14.131
I got an interview with for that class.

00:34:14.131 --> 00:34:15.361
When you already get to the interview,

00:34:15.361 --> 00:34:17.843
there are thousands of people that apply,

00:34:17.843 --> 00:34:20.488
and they typically would
interview about 120 people

00:34:20.488 --> 00:34:22.251
over the course of a few months.

00:34:22.251 --> 00:34:24.268
But that's a pretty big cut.

00:34:24.268 --> 00:34:27.092
And they'd come in for the physical,

00:34:27.092 --> 00:34:29.973
and a lot of people were getting DQed

00:34:29.973 --> 00:34:31.973
because of the eye test.

00:34:32.807 --> 00:34:34.349
So that's what happened to me,

00:34:34.349 --> 00:34:36.254
and I said, "Well, there's
no way to get around this."

00:34:36.254 --> 00:34:38.237
And then I was pretty disappointed.

00:34:38.237 --> 00:34:39.906
But then I started asking questions.

00:34:39.906 --> 00:34:42.210
Bob Overmyer was a Marine Corps pilot

00:34:42.210 --> 00:34:44.364
who was my boss at McDonnell Douglas.

00:34:44.364 --> 00:34:45.279
And he said, "How'd it go?"

00:34:45.279 --> 00:34:46.348
And I said, "I got DQed."

00:34:46.348 --> 00:34:47.181
And he goes, "It was your eyes?"

00:34:47.181 --> 00:34:48.014
And I said, "Yeah."

00:34:48.014 --> 00:34:48.847
And he goes, "You know
what you got to do."

00:34:48.847 --> 00:34:49.680
I said, "No."

00:34:49.680 --> 00:34:51.129
He said, "You got to dehydrate yourself

00:34:51.129 --> 00:34:52.477
before you take that test."

00:34:52.477 --> 00:34:53.396
I was like, "Really?

00:34:53.396 --> 00:34:54.229
He said, "Yeah."

00:34:54.229 --> 00:34:55.062
I go, "Why is that?"

00:34:55.062 --> 00:34:55.895
He goes, "Well, you know,

00:34:55.895 --> 00:34:56.737
you get all the water out of the eye,

00:34:56.737 --> 00:34:59.233
and it flattens the eye,
and it focuses differently."

00:34:59.233 --> 00:35:00.425
And he goes, "That's what I used to do."

00:35:00.425 --> 00:35:01.609
He's a Marine Corps pilot, you know?

00:35:01.609 --> 00:35:02.823
And he goes, "You know,
you should try that."

00:35:02.823 --> 00:35:04.774
And I go, "Oh, OK, maybe
there's something there."

00:35:04.774 --> 00:35:07.827
Next couple hours, that afternoon,

00:35:07.827 --> 00:35:09.127
at the Johnson Space Center,

00:35:09.127 --> 00:35:10.403
I'm walking through the hallway,

00:35:10.403 --> 00:35:12.418
my friend Kevin Kregel is there,

00:35:12.418 --> 00:35:14.866
and he's an Air Force test
pilot who's an astronaut.

00:35:14.866 --> 00:35:15.869
And he goes, "How'd it go?"

00:35:15.869 --> 00:35:16.702
"I got DQed."

00:35:16.702 --> 00:35:17.535
He goes, "Your eyesight?"

00:35:17.535 --> 00:35:18.618
I go, "Yeah."

00:35:19.493 --> 00:35:20.542
He goes, "You know what
you got to do, don't you?"

00:35:20.542 --> 00:35:21.375
And I go, "What's that?"

00:35:21.375 --> 00:35:22.712
He goes, "You got to
drink a lot of water."

00:35:22.712 --> 00:35:24.531
Can you imagine?

00:35:24.531 --> 00:35:25.620
You can't make this up.

00:35:25.620 --> 00:35:26.664
I'm like, "What?"

00:35:26.664 --> 00:35:28.298
And he says, "Oh, it makes
the eye more viscous,

00:35:28.298 --> 00:35:29.321
and it bends the light."

00:35:29.321 --> 00:35:31.397
And I'm like, "Well, no one
knows what they're doing here."

00:35:31.397 --> 00:35:35.147
So maybe there's some
way to get around this.

00:35:37.065 --> 00:35:39.241
I don't know if LASIK existed,

00:35:39.241 --> 00:35:40.864
but probably not even then.

00:35:40.864 --> 00:35:42.532
But there were, like,
procedures you could have done,

00:35:42.532 --> 00:35:45.855
and that was lifetime-banning.

00:35:45.855 --> 00:35:47.823
So that wasn't a good idea.

00:35:47.823 --> 00:35:49.674
When I saw the astronaut job,

00:35:49.674 --> 00:35:52.204
that was to me the
opportunity of a lifetime.

00:35:52.204 --> 00:35:54.287
And I was not going to stop.

00:35:55.586 --> 00:35:59.586
Even if I couldn't get
the medical issue solved,

00:36:02.151 --> 00:36:04.012
I would keep trying to get it solved.

00:36:04.012 --> 00:36:05.721
I wasn't going to give up.

00:36:05.721 --> 00:36:07.847
I found out about this training

00:36:07.847 --> 00:36:10.446
in this NASA flight docs,

00:36:10.446 --> 00:36:11.451
you know, the doctors were like,

00:36:11.451 --> 00:36:12.870
"Well, we think it's hocus-pocus

00:36:12.870 --> 00:36:14.136
because, you know,

00:36:14.136 --> 00:36:16.324
you just can't really
train your eyes to do that,

00:36:16.324 --> 00:36:17.715
but go ahead, you know?

00:36:17.715 --> 00:36:18.548
We don't care.

00:36:18.548 --> 00:36:20.046
It doesn't do anything to your eye."

00:36:20.046 --> 00:36:23.048
And the doctor had told me that,

00:36:23.048 --> 00:36:25.717
the eye doctor did, it was
only going to work with kids.

00:36:25.717 --> 00:36:26.956
So she didn't think it was going to work.

00:36:26.956 --> 00:36:29.047
But I told her I could be really immature.

00:36:29.047 --> 00:36:29.928
And then she said, "OK."

00:36:29.928 --> 00:36:30.761
You know, she helped me,

00:36:30.761 --> 00:36:31.997
and I was able to pick up a couple lines

00:36:31.997 --> 00:36:33.744
just so I could try again.

00:36:33.744 --> 00:36:35.600
The eye exercise was kind of,

00:36:35.600 --> 00:36:38.235
it was kind of like trying
to learn how to focus

00:36:38.235 --> 00:36:39.891
beyond what you were looking at.

00:36:39.891 --> 00:36:42.348
You're kind of training your eyes to relax

00:36:42.348 --> 00:36:45.415
and almost focus beyond
what they're looking at.

00:36:45.415 --> 00:36:47.337
And then the letter or the number

00:36:47.337 --> 00:36:49.777
or whatever it's that you're
trying to see off a chart

00:36:49.777 --> 00:36:51.375
will come into view.

00:36:51.375 --> 00:36:53.270
And it's also other things you can do

00:36:53.270 --> 00:36:56.655
like wearing under-corrected lenses

00:36:56.655 --> 00:36:58.964
so that your eyes are
working a little bit more.

00:36:58.964 --> 00:37:01.238
It's like training the brain to just relax

00:37:01.238 --> 00:37:02.871
and kind of look beyond
what it's looking at

00:37:02.871 --> 00:37:04.148
to try to have things

00:37:04.148 --> 00:37:06.772
that normally wouldn't be
in focus come into focus.

00:37:11.620 --> 00:37:14.139
I am really excited about the moon.

00:37:14.139 --> 00:37:16.763
I've been hoping we've sent people there

00:37:16.763 --> 00:37:18.750
since I became an
astronaut, even before that.

00:37:18.750 --> 00:37:21.737
I was down at the launch
for the Artemis II flight,

00:37:21.737 --> 00:37:26.380
and the Kennedy Space Center
is unbelievable right now.

00:37:26.380 --> 00:37:28.282
And I think to realize that,

00:37:28.282 --> 00:37:30.760
you had needed to have been
there before, like years ago.

00:37:30.760 --> 00:37:32.939
What's happened in the last few years

00:37:32.939 --> 00:37:35.710
is now you have these
launches of satellites.

00:37:35.710 --> 00:37:37.933
Sometimes you have two
launches in one day.

00:37:37.933 --> 00:37:41.133
You know, over a hundred
launches last year.

00:37:41.133 --> 00:37:42.785
With all the communication satellites

00:37:42.785 --> 00:37:44.618
and some space probes,

00:37:45.589 --> 00:37:47.348
mainly communication satellite's going up

00:37:47.348 --> 00:37:48.655
and then crews are going up,

00:37:48.655 --> 00:37:51.160
not all that often, but
a couple times a year.

00:37:51.160 --> 00:37:52.211
They are building things.

00:37:52.211 --> 00:37:55.236
Blue Origin has a humongous
building down there

00:37:55.236 --> 00:37:57.491
and they're building a
vehicle assembly building.

00:37:57.491 --> 00:38:00.150
SpaceX is for their giant Starship.

00:38:00.150 --> 00:38:00.983
It's huge.

00:38:00.983 --> 00:38:02.027
It looks futuristic,

00:38:02.027 --> 00:38:04.128
all the new buildings
that are coming online

00:38:04.128 --> 00:38:07.295
and the launchpads that they're using.

00:38:08.214 --> 00:38:09.047
And they're using them.

00:38:09.047 --> 00:38:10.311
I mean it's like there's launches

00:38:10.311 --> 00:38:12.151
almost every day down there.

00:38:12.151 --> 00:38:15.369
So it's almost like a
futuristic space port down there

00:38:15.369 --> 00:38:16.938
and it's just growing.

00:38:16.938 --> 00:38:19.037
It's going to get more and more active.

00:38:19.037 --> 00:38:22.388
So I think there's a lot there.

00:38:22.388 --> 00:38:24.094
We might hear about the Artemis missions

00:38:24.094 --> 00:38:26.980
because those are very
interesting to people.

00:38:26.980 --> 00:38:28.130
People go into the moon,

00:38:28.130 --> 00:38:30.261
but there's a lot of launches going on.

00:38:30.261 --> 00:38:33.785
Still our launches going to
the International Space Station

00:38:33.785 --> 00:38:34.618
with crews,

00:38:34.618 --> 00:38:37.517
and I think the future is going to be...

00:38:37.517 --> 00:38:40.237
The International Space
Station low Earth orbit,

00:38:40.237 --> 00:38:42.383
the hope is that'll get turned over

00:38:42.383 --> 00:38:45.620
to commercial space stations to utilize.

00:38:45.620 --> 00:38:48.756
When I started teaching 13 years ago,

00:38:48.756 --> 00:38:50.972
you know, it was starting to get started,

00:38:50.972 --> 00:38:55.402
but we weren't sending people
to space on commercial rockets

00:38:55.402 --> 00:38:56.302
and now we are.

00:38:56.302 --> 00:38:59.090
Commercial space flight
really didn't exist that much.

00:38:59.090 --> 00:39:00.182
A lot has changed.

00:39:00.182 --> 00:39:02.030
They were talking about all these
things they were going to do.

00:39:02.030 --> 00:39:05.250
The commercial companies and
bringing the booster back

00:39:05.250 --> 00:39:06.984
and using it again and landing it.

00:39:06.984 --> 00:39:10.424
It was like, "Oh, see what happens."

00:39:10.424 --> 00:39:13.288
When I saw the first one of those landing

00:39:13.288 --> 00:39:14.216
back at the launchpad

00:39:14.216 --> 00:39:18.595
and then landing it on a barge
out in the ocean at night,

00:39:18.595 --> 00:39:19.428
that is incredible.

00:39:19.428 --> 00:39:20.540
So I was really impressed with that.

00:39:20.540 --> 00:39:24.200
The reusability of it is what's
going to make it affordable.

00:39:24.200 --> 00:39:27.986
The commercial space sector
that has grown immensely

00:39:27.986 --> 00:39:29.924
over the past 12 years or so.

00:39:29.924 --> 00:39:31.869
And I think that's probably
the biggest difference

00:39:31.869 --> 00:39:33.260
between what was going on

00:39:33.260 --> 00:39:35.565
and what I'm talking
about in the classroom

00:39:35.565 --> 00:39:38.026
compared to all those years ago.

00:39:38.026 --> 00:39:40.903
The NASA program itself,
we've kind of cut back.

00:39:40.903 --> 00:39:43.072
There was more astronauts
when I was there,

00:39:43.072 --> 00:39:46.223
and now there are fewer astronauts
who were on flight status

00:39:46.223 --> 00:39:48.287
because there's not as many
of them going into space.

00:39:48.287 --> 00:39:51.140
Hopefully that cadence will pick up a bit

00:39:51.140 --> 00:39:52.603
now that we're going to the moon

00:39:52.603 --> 00:39:55.757
and we're still going to
the space station right now.

00:39:55.757 --> 00:39:57.402
Some of the research
that's been done in space

00:39:57.402 --> 00:39:59.871
on the space station
has really come around.

00:39:59.871 --> 00:40:01.923
They've been able to use
a microgravity environment

00:40:01.923 --> 00:40:04.857
for some great research
and commercial activities,

00:40:04.857 --> 00:40:07.357
but it's a bit dangerous
sending humans to these places.

00:40:07.357 --> 00:40:10.755
So whenever you can, use a robotic device.

00:40:10.755 --> 00:40:12.436
I think that's a good idea.

00:40:12.436 --> 00:40:14.750
I think there's a still a practical reason

00:40:14.750 --> 00:40:16.568
for people to go to space.

00:40:16.568 --> 00:40:19.135
That is we're able to do things that...

00:40:19.135 --> 00:40:21.284
Still, we're able to do
things that robots can't do

00:40:21.284 --> 00:40:22.643
or even with AI.

00:40:22.643 --> 00:40:25.060
We can make decisions based on experience.

00:40:25.060 --> 00:40:26.761
For example, on a planetary surface,

00:40:26.761 --> 00:40:28.795
especially the rovers on Mars,

00:40:28.795 --> 00:40:31.217
you know, they don't go very fast

00:40:31.217 --> 00:40:32.855
and don't carry a lot of ground.

00:40:32.855 --> 00:40:34.377
Over like a period of 10 years,

00:40:34.377 --> 00:40:35.776
you can get with maybe,

00:40:35.776 --> 00:40:37.784
I don't know what the
exact correlation would be,

00:40:37.784 --> 00:40:39.993
but in much less than that time.

00:40:39.993 --> 00:40:41.337
If you had a person walking around,

00:40:41.337 --> 00:40:43.157
you could cover that same ground.

00:40:48.075 --> 00:40:50.487
I'm a professor at Columbia
Mechanical Engineering

00:40:50.487 --> 00:40:54.539
and I teach classes that
are related to space flight,

00:40:54.539 --> 00:40:56.331
introduction to human space flight,

00:40:56.331 --> 00:40:57.292
aerospace human factors.

00:40:57.292 --> 00:41:01.025
I teach art of engineering,
which is for new students.

00:41:01.025 --> 00:41:02.763
And for the mechanical
engineering section,

00:41:02.763 --> 00:41:03.676
we build rockets.

00:41:03.676 --> 00:41:05.895
We just launched them last weekend.

00:41:05.895 --> 00:41:08.015
That was kind of fun.

00:41:08.015 --> 00:41:09.082
So I teach those classes.

00:41:09.082 --> 00:41:11.347
I advise the space club,
which is quite active.

00:41:11.347 --> 00:41:13.626
We have 500 students.

00:41:13.626 --> 00:41:17.178
About 250 or so are active
doing different projects

00:41:17.178 --> 00:41:19.684
from rockets to space flight experiments

00:41:19.684 --> 00:41:22.483
to high-altitude balloons
to all kinds of things.

00:41:22.483 --> 00:41:23.715
So that's pretty enjoyable.

00:41:23.715 --> 00:41:26.356
We're starting an aerospace program there.

00:41:26.356 --> 00:41:27.189
We have a minor,

00:41:27.189 --> 00:41:29.260
and we're starting a major
in aerospace engineering.

00:41:29.260 --> 00:41:31.425
So it's taking the first steps for that.

00:41:31.425 --> 00:41:32.660
Now I've got my first book.

00:41:32.660 --> 00:41:36.031
"Spaceman" is more of a memoir,
and then, it is a memoir.

00:41:36.031 --> 00:41:38.087
New York Times bestseller, did well.

00:41:38.087 --> 00:41:42.098
I also have a junior, like a
young reader version of that.

00:41:42.098 --> 00:41:43.843
And then I have another book, "Moonshot,"

00:41:43.843 --> 00:41:46.010
which came out more recently.

00:41:46.010 --> 00:41:49.513
It's kind of like my top 10
lessons learned as an astronaut.

00:41:49.513 --> 00:41:51.810
It's more in the self-help space.

00:41:51.810 --> 00:41:53.558
If NASA calls me tomorrow
to go to the moon,

00:41:53.558 --> 00:41:54.998
my answer is yes,

00:41:54.998 --> 00:41:57.863
but I can guarantee you
they won't be calling.

00:41:57.863 --> 00:42:02.149
So I'm going to be staying
here, I think, on the planet.

00:42:02.149 --> 00:42:04.583
Hi, I'm a producer on Authorized Account.

00:42:04.583 --> 00:42:05.864
If you enjoyed this video,

00:42:05.864 --> 00:42:08.343
then please subscribe and
comment with more topics

00:42:08.343 --> 00:42:11.926
that you'd like us to
cover in this series.

